    <name>
      <text>Trigonometric Ratios Using Compound-Angle Formulae</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>
Consider the following trigonometric expression: \(\sin({@A@}^\circ+{@B@}^\circ)\)
</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> Use the compound-angle identity for sine to expand the expression.</p>

<p>[[input:ans0]]</p>
<p>[[validation:ans0]]</p>

<p>
 Hence, determine the exact value of \(\sin({@A@}^\circ+{@B@}^\circ)\)
</p>



<p>[[input:ans1]]</p>
<p>[[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p><strong>Instructions:</strong> Give your answers in exact form. Do not use decimal approximations.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>

<p>We are required to evaluate \(\sin({@A@}^\circ+{@B@}^\circ)\)</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Recall the compound-angle identity.</strong></p>

<p>\(\sin(A+B)=\sin A\cos B+\cos A\sin B\)</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Substitute the given angles.</strong></p>

<p>\(\sin({@A@}^\circ+{@B@}^\circ)\)</p>

<p>
\(=\sin{@A@}^\circ\cos{@B@}^\circ+\cos{@A@}^\circ\sin{@B@}^\circ\)
</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Substitute the standard trigonometric values.</strong></p>

<p>Using the exact values of the sine and cosine ratios for the standard angles, we obtain:</p>

<p>\(\sin({@A@}^\circ+{@B@}^\circ)=\frac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4}\)</p>

<p><strong>Final Answer:</strong></p>

<p>\(\boxed{\frac{\sqrt6+\sqrt2}{4}}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
